    {
      "name": "Severity Chip",
      "kind": "chip",
      "refersTo": "chip-severity-high",
      "description": "Functional condition rating: bg/fg pair, 2px corner, always with a label.",
      "html": "<span class=\"ds-sev\">High</span>",
      "css": ".ds-sev{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;font:600 .875rem/1 Inter,system-ui,sans-serif;padding:.32em .7em;border-radius:2px;background:#FFEDD5;color:#9A3412}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Condition Finding Card",
      "kind": "card",
      "refersTo": "card-finding",
      "description": "White plane, hairline edge, no shadow: chip, title, slate body, tracked-caps reference.",
      "html": "<article class=\"ds-card\"><span class=\"ds-sev-c\">High</span><h4>Ravelling and ponding at the gate approach</h4><p>Loose stone and standing water where trucks turn in. A candidate for the trial area.</p><span class=\"ds-label\">Condition finding 03 · Gate approach</span></article>",
      "css": ".ds-card{background:#FFFFFF;border:1px solid rgba(38,40,43,.14);padding:24px;display:grid;gap:12px;max-width:420px;font-family:Inter,system-ui,sans-serif;color:#5C6670}.ds-card h4{margin:0;font:700 1.125rem/1.2 Figtree,system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#26282B}.ds-card p{margin:0;font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.6}.ds-sev-c{justify-self:start;font:600 .875rem/1 Inter,system-ui,sans-serif;padding:.32em .7em;border-radius:2px;background:#FFEDD5;color:#9A3412}.ds-label{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.9em;font:600 .6875rem/1.4 Inter,system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.24em;text-transform:uppercase}.ds-label::before{content:\"\";width:28px;height:2px;background:currentColor;flex:none}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Figure Panel",
      "kind": "custom",
      "refersTo": "panel-figure",
      "description": "Charcoal panel, one huge figure, a label, and the qualifier that always travels with it.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-fig\"><div class=\"ds-fig-n\">60–70%</div><span class=\"ds-label\">Indicatively below engineered concrete hardstand</span><p>Indicative. Varies with base material, climate and duty. Confirmed by site trial.</p></div>",
      "css": ".ds-fig{background:#26282B;color:#FFFFFF;padding:48px 32px;display:grid;gap:16px;max-width:460px;font-family:Inter,system-ui,sans-serif}.ds-fig-n{font:800 clamp(3.5rem,9vw,6rem)/.95 Figtree,system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.02em;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.ds-fig p{margin:0;font-size:.75rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.55)}.ds-label{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.9em;font:600 .6875rem/1.4 Inter,system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.24em;text-transform:uppercase}.ds-label::before{content:\"\";width:28px;height:2px;background:currentColor;flex:none}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Two-Tone Call-out",
      "kind": "custom",
      "refersTo": "callout",
      "description": "Panel block with one two-tone statement: grey setup, white payoff.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-call\"><p>The choice is usually “keep making it good” or “pour a slab”. <strong>There is a third option.</strong></p></div>",
      "css": ".ds-call{background:#2F3233;padding:48px 32px;max-width:520px}.ds-call p{margin:0;font:800 clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)/1.12 Figtree,system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:#99A1A7}.ds-call strong{color:#FFFFFF;font-weight:inherit}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Standing Ground",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"Standing Ground\"**\n\nMaintain Pavements builds bound hardstand out of the yard base a site already has: dosed, mixed through the full depth, graded to level, rolled and compacted. The brand looks like the finished pad: level, dense, quietly engineered, done. Its name comes from Jon Pepper's own line, \"the base you already have, turned into standing ground\".\n\nThe system is one colour in two states. Charcoal `#26282B`, the ground of the chevron mark, is the brand: white on charcoal, charcoal on paper. Everything else is the greys of the page, hairline rules and very large numbers. There is no accent colour. Signal comes from the inversion itself, and colour comes from the site photography: hi-vis, plant yellow, red soil, blue sky. The layout is editorial and generous, in the manner of a capability statement rather than an app: wide margins, sharp edges, tracked-caps captions, numbered steps, and headlines that set up in grey and pay off in white.\n\nThe voice matches the look: measured, not asserted. Every figure travels with its qualifier.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One brand colour, charcoal `#26282B`, used in two states; no accent.",
      "Figtree 800 two-tone headlines over Inter body; tracked-caps labels.",
      "Sharp corners (2px ceiling on small controls), hairline rules, flat surfaces.",
      "Numbers as heroes: the method's 01–05 and the big-number panel.",
      "Surveyed terrain art toned onto the charcoal ramp; real site photography first."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two States Rule",
        "body": "Charcoal on paper and white; white on charcoal. There is no third state. Charcoal on the dark grounds is 1.1:1, so the logo and any charcoal shape invert to white there.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Accent Rule",
        "body": "No brand hue exists beyond charcoal. The one primary action on a surface is made by inversion: white with charcoal ink on a dark ground, charcoal with white ink on a light one. If a surface seems to need colour, use a photograph.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Grey Setup Rule",
        "body": "Mid grey #99A1A7 is a dark-ground colour. On paper or white the setup line of a two-tone headline is slate #5C6670 (`.mp-two-tone.on-light`).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two-Tone Rule",
        "body": "A display headline may set up in grey and pay off in white (or slate and charcoal on light): \"Stop patching the yard. **Bind it.**\" One payoff per headline, and the payoff is always the shorter half.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Label Is Not an Eyebrow Rule",
        "body": "The tracked-caps label captions things: photos, footage, table columns, steps. It never sits stacked above a headline; the headline carries its own weight.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Margin Is the Material Rule",
        "body": "Generous margins and hairlines do the structural work that boxes and fills would do elsewhere. When in doubt, add space, not a container.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Pad Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces at rest carry no shadow. A card is a white plane with a hairline edge, not a lifted tile.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Pixel Ceiling Rule",
        "body": "No radius above 2px anywhere, on anything.",
        "section": "shapes"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do build against the semantic roles (`--color-primary`, `--color-text`, `--color-setup`…), never raw hex; raw `--mp-*` values only define new roles.",
      "Do invert, never recolour: white on charcoal (14.8:1), charcoal on paper (13.4:1) and white (14.8:1).",
      "Do keep one primary action per surface.",
      "Do set the two-tone setup in mid grey #99A1A7 on dark and in slate #5C6670 on light.",
      "Do attach the qualifier to every figure: \"Indicative. Varies with base material, climate and duty. Confirmed by site trial.\"",
      "Do keep corners square, with 2px only on buttons, inputs and chips.",
      "Do contrast-check any new colour pair with `python design-system/contrast.py` and record it in `tokens.json` and here."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't introduce an accent colour, amber, hi-vis orange or otherwise, as a UI colour. Colour belongs to the photography.",
      "Don't place the charcoal logo or charcoal shapes on a dark ground (1.1:1).",
      "Don't use mid grey #99A1A7 as text on paper or white (2.4–2.6:1).",
      "Don't use a radius above 2px, pill shapes, or drop shadows on resting cards.",
      "Don't use gradient fills or gradient text in UI. Gradients live only inside the imagery.",
      "Don't stack a tracked-caps label above a headline as an eyebrow.",
      "Don't set PAVEMENTS, or any part of the lockup, in a typeface."
    ]
  }
}